 "note": "Found by the nightly fuzz run on 2026-11-10, at 6000 generated pumping cases. A constant test at 0.19 L/s whose drawdown sits at 35 to 41 cm for 100 minutes, a rise of about two centimetres under reading noise of as much: Cooper-Jacob is refused, and the Theis fit absorbs the offset into a storativity of 9e-35 and is adopted at T = 300 m2/day. The two fits were 2e-7 apart, 300.13210 in Python (scipy's curve_fit) and 300.13216 in the browser (its own Levenberg-Marquardt), each where its own stopping rule left it on a flat valley floor. The bootstrap refits from the fit, and one of its 400 refits walks the valley to a storativity near 1e-280, the floor a refit is refused below. It stopped at 10^-279.4 from the one start and would have reached 10^-280.2 from the other, so Python counted 358 replicates and the browser 357, and with one value more in a sparse upper tail P90 came out at 642 m2/day against 629. Neither engine's arithmetic was wrong; neither fit was finished. Three changes, in both engines unless said: Python's theis_fit takes the browser's search step for step instead of calling curve_fit; a Theis fit is finished with Newton steps on its closed-form derivatives (spread.polish_log2, polishLog2), which reach the minimum to 1e-12 where a search that watches the misfit stops at 1e-8; and each bootstrap refit of a Theis fit is finished the same way. On this sheet the fits now agree to 4e-15 (300.13216294624) and the band to 1e-14 (155 to 629 m2/day, from 357 replicates in both).",
